{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1545138-a-sci-fi-scenario-book-which-scenarios","id":1545138,"title":"A Sci Fi Scenario Book, which Scenarios?","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Mage: The Ascension"],"posts":[{"date":"2024-11-01T09:01:22-07:00","id":1545138,"title":"A Sci Fi Scenario Book, which Scenarios?","content":"I know we aren't scheduled for anymore books period, but this is a fantasy. Picture we get a book with five Sci Fi settings for M:tA. It's a thick book, 280 pages. Each scenario is to get 50 pages and the frame and supporting tools get the other 30.<br /> <br /> The question is &quot;Which Scenarios?&quot;<br /> <br /> Myself, I see the basic scenarios for a Sci Fi rpg setting as, A) Cyberpunk, B) Post-Apocalyptic, C) Exploratory Space Opera a couple of centuries from the present (think Star Trek, but also Alien ), D) Space Opera a millennium or more onward (think Dune, Traveller RPG), and E) Time Travel/Time Wars.<br /> <br /> But how do you see the possibilities? Would you work with my categories or swap them out for other ideas? Some of these settings are traditionally gritty, others tilt glam. Would you lean into that of flip the script?<br /> <br /> Please give your visions and fancies.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":857,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/857-astromancer","user":"Astromancer","date":". 11-01-2024, 12:04 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/857-astromancer","name":"Astromancer","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar857_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2195"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-11-01T16:04:42-07:00","id":1545167,"title":"","content":"I think the biggest question is what would make these Mage settings as opposed to just general scifi settings? There is also the issue of the two most obvious examples, cyberpunk and space opera, essentially being built around single Traditions. What do Ecstatics or Hermetics do in something based on Star Trek, for instance? That is something any setting would need to answer, at least IMO.<br /> <br /> That said, one possibility is an Afrofuturism setting. <br /> <br /> Another is something akin to the novel Celestial Matters, in which the entire laws of physics and cosmology are different, reflecting Hellenic Age ideas about an earth-centric universe.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-11-01T18:15:32-07:00","id":1545182,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>No One of Consequence</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1545138-a-sci-fi-scenario-book-which-scenarios?p=1545167#post1545167\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I think the biggest question is what would make these Mage settings as opposed to just general scifi settings? There is also the issue of the two most obvious examples, cyberpunk and space opera, essentially being built around single Traditions. What do Ecstatics or Hermetics do in something based on Star Trek, for instance?</div> </div> </div> </div>Ecstatics? Probably what Kirk does, but with more drugs. Hermetics? Continue to build towers on unexplored worlds and maintain their purloined traditions of wizardly magic, acting very smug when the Enterprise lands on a planet but they already have a tower there. So... a lot like that TOS episode with Apollo.<br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23-saur-ops-specialist","name":"Saur Ops Specialist","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9371"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-11-02T04:43:18-07:00","id":1545207,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>No One of Consequence</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1545138-a-sci-fi-scenario-book-which-scenarios?p=1545167#post1545167\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I think the biggest question is what would make these Mage settings as opposed to just general scifi settings? There is also the issue of the two most obvious examples, cyberpunk and space opera, essentially being built around single Traditions. What do Ecstatics or Hermetics do in something based on Star Trek, for instance? That is something any setting would need to answer, at least IMO.</div> </div> </div> </div>I did say those five were basic. And I think just about all Sci Fi rpg settings could be placed in one of those categories. Making a distinct setting would be the goal/challenge.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> That said, one possibility is an Afrofuturism setting. </div> </div> </div>Perhaps set after a three-way struggle between the Technocoracy, the Trads, and the Cammarilla, destroyed or crippled the West. The Hollow Ones would have fallen out with the other Desparates and would be working to restore the West. The Ngoma would be focused on building African prosperity. The Batini and the Wu Lung striving to rule the world alone. A Desparates in charge world.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Another is something akin to the novel Celestial Matters, in which the entire laws of physics and cosmology are different, reflecting Hellenic Age ideas about an earth-centric universe. </div> </div> </div>Sounds fun.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/857-astromancer","name":"Astromancer","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar857_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2195"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-11-02T04:44:33-07:00","id":1545208,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Saur Ops Specialist</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1545138-a-sci-fi-scenario-book-which-scenarios?p=1545182#post1545182\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Ecstatics? Probably what Kirk does, but with more drugs. Hermetics? Continue to build towers on unexplored worlds and maintain their purloined traditions of wizardly magic, acting very smug when the Enterprise lands on a planet but they already have a tower there. So... a lot like that TOS episode with Apollo.<br /></div> </div> </div> </div>Agreed. If the setting is big enough, all the archetypes fit nicely.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/857-astromancer","name":"Astromancer","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar857_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2195"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-11-02T08:51:49-07:00","id":1545210,"title":"","content":"I remembered a twist I once did on both Cyberpunk and Post-Apocalyptic. This twist was inspired by the film Cherry-2000. Basically, there were surviving cities in the Post-Apocalyptic world. These cities were basically Cyberpunk cities as fortresses. Within the cities you have decadence and oppression. Outside of the cities you had savagery and desolation. In a Mage/WoD setting the Cammarilla and the Technocoracy would be struggling for control of the cities. The Garou and the Fera would be struggling to control the countryside. The Trads and the Changelings would have strongholds in the countryside and the edges of the cities. <br /> <br /> Radicals among the Technocoracy, allied with the Trads and others would be working to restore civilization on a healthy basis. The power elites would fight this in order to retain their power. <br /> <br /> But that's just one twist.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/857-astromancer","name":"Astromancer","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar857_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2195"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-11-02T18:24:43-07:00","id":1545239,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Astromancer</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1545138-a-sci-fi-scenario-book-which-scenarios?p=1545210#post1545210\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I remembered a twist I once did on both Cyberpunk and Post-Apocalyptic. This twist was inspired by the film Cherry-2000. Basically, there were surviving cities in the Post-Apocalyptic world. These cities were basically Cyberpunk cities as fortresses. Within the cities you have decadence and oppression. Outside of the cities you had savagery and desolation. In a Mage/WoD setting the Cammarilla and the Technocoracy would be struggling for control of the cities. <b>The Garou and the Fera would be struggling to control the countryside</b>. The Trads and the Changelings would have strongholds in the countryside and the edges of the cities.<br /> <br /> Radicals among the Technocoracy, allied with the Trads and others would be working to restore civilization on a healthy basis. The power elites would fight this in order to retain their power.<br /> <br /> But that's just one twist.</div> </div> </div> </div>What of the Garou and Fera that hang around in the cities? Bone Gnawers are cyberpunk classics, able to make junk work even when it shouldn't, and Glass Walkers can make the most hardened cybersoldier play the game where you have to (smack) quit hitting yourself...","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23-saur-ops-specialist","name":"Saur Ops Specialist","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9371"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-11-03T03:20:20-08:00","id":1545242,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Saur Ops Specialist</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1545138-a-sci-fi-scenario-book-which-scenarios?p=1545239#post1545239\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> What of the Garou and Fera that hang around in the cities? Bone Gnawers are cyberpunk classics, able to make junk work even when it shouldn't, and Glass Walkers can make the most hardened cybersoldier play the game where you have to (smack) quit hitting yourself...</div> </div> </div> </div>They'd certainly be there, but the Cam and the Technos would be the dominant factions. For the trads they'd be more of an issue fighting for the cearns/nexus points on the outskirts of the cities.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/857-astromancer","name":"Astromancer","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar857_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2195"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-11-04T09:10:29-08:00","id":1545316,"title":"","content":"I think an important element in any book like this is stepping out of the current Ascension war. The current balance of the Ascension War (Trad vs Tech, the current traditions and conventions, freedom vs safety, culture vs progress, etc.) is not baked into the setting. Each scenario can and should choose an Ascension Conflict that relates to the themes of that setting. If we do keep the Traditions and Convensions and goals that we currently have it should be because the scenario offers a fresh and interesting take on what we thought we knew.<br /> <br /> The only two that seem capable of that, to me, are the Time Wars and (strangely) the Cyberpunk settings. The Time Travel game has the most potential to dig into the meat of what each group believes, what they've done, and what shaped them. Cyberpunk, meanwhile, gives the opportunity to see them in action on a dramatically different world and how they would function with different worldly rules.<br /> <br /> Now, in one sense, all of the scenarios allow that, but we really only need to hit that idea once. After that it becomes stale. So I'd recommend picking one to carry that water. Of all of them I think Cyberpunk and Near Future Space Opera do it best. NFSO has a lot more potential to spread its wings, though, so I'd stick with Cyberpunk.<br /> <br /> For the remaining three, I'd recommend coming up with completely different struggles. Indeed, I'd recommend one that doesn't have an Ascension War per se (post apocalyptic would be a good choice) and one where the nature of the Ascension War is public knowledge (FFSO would work well).","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Mage: The Ice-ension: An Epic Game of Reality on the Rink","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/758-ramnesis","name":"Ramnesis","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar758_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2220"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-11-06T12:53:45-08:00","id":1545488,"title":"","content":"Digging into cyberpunk a little.<br /> <br /> Cyberpunk on the surface seems very Technocratic, but often in a pyrrhic sense. Technology and business are in ascendency, but usually beneath the surface things are in decay. Unintended consequences abound, especially when it comes to the new technology, and things are often put to uses they were never intended to.<br /> <br /> So what I see here is a world where the Technocracy was winning, and then they brought fully sapient AIs and androids (complete with Avatars) into the Consensus. This revolutionized the world, but the Technocracy never accounted for Citizen 2.0 being just as opinionated and uncontrollable as Citizen 1.0. They fell into disarray as their timetable was rapidly made obsolete.<br /> It's been a generation since then. On the Technocracy side, they appear to have regained control, but much of that is in appearance. In reality they are fighting an uphill battle to reassert their plans. It's a struggle, but one they intend to win.<br /> <br /> On the Traditions side, things look very different. There's a generational struggle between the old guard who are trying to preserve their practices from the world and the new guard who are adapting to it. Overall, though, they are going strong, and while some members are uncomfortable with it, almost every Tradition now has AI members.<br /> <br /> This isn't a setting of VAs vs Technocracy. Instead everybody is using AI and Hacking the same way everyone in the standard setting uses cell phones and lightbulbs. <br /> <br /> That's my rough crack at the idea, anyways.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> Two other scenario ideas:<br /> <br /> Pulp and Dinosaur Diesel Punk.<br /> <br /> I'm realizing that second one can be taken multiple ways. I meant it as actual intelligent dinosaur people in their own prehistoric industrial setting, but it also works as human dinosaur riders in a prehistoric industrial setting.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Mage: The Ice-ension: An Epic Game of Reality on the Rink","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/758-ramnesis","name":"Ramnesis","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar758_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2220"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-11-06T14:45:26-08:00","id":1545491,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <br /> Two other scenario ideas:<br /> <br /> Pulp and Dinosaur Diesel Punk.<br /> <br /> I'm realizing that second one can be taken multiple ways. I meant it as actual intelligent dinosaur people in their own prehistoric industrial setting, but it also works as human dinosaur riders in a prehistoric industrial setting. </div> </div> </div>I like <b>Xenozoic Tales </b>too. And it also blends, in a limited but lively way, Cyberpunk and Post-Apocalyptic.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/857-astromancer","name":"Astromancer","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar857_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2195"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-11-06T14:57:57-08:00","id":1545492,"title":"","content":"A Near Future Space Opera (NFSO) that blended <b>Star Trek </b>with <b>Alien</b>, not literally though, would be a good choice. A setting were Idealism and Cynicism clashed on near equal terms could make great <b>Mage </b>gaming. Within one society in Known Space and Idealistic and Honorable cultural movement (basically the Federation) is squaring off against a decaying corperate elite. Automation, A.I., Robotics, fusion power generation, replicators, asteroid mining and other technologies make a functional socialism work. The Corporate Elites, realizing that this would be a repeat of the introduction of democracy in the West, a long slow retreat for kings and dictators, fear that success for the Idealists will mean the erasure of their way of life. <br /> <br /> It's a subtle war for reality in the vast expanses of space.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/857-astromancer","name":"Astromancer","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar857_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2195"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-11-07T09:10:14-08:00","id":1545523,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Astromancer</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1545138-a-sci-fi-scenario-book-which-scenarios?p=1545492#post1545492\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">A Near Future Space Opera (NFSO) that blended <b>Star Trek </b>with <b>Alien</b>, not literally though, would be a good choice. A setting were Idealism and Cynicism clashed on near equal terms could make great <b>Mage </b>gaming. Within one society in Known Space and Idealistic and Honorable cultural movement (basically the Federation) is squaring off against a decaying corperate elite. Automation, A.I., Robotics, fusion power generation, replicators, asteroid mining and other technologies make a functional socialism work. The Corporate Elites, realizing that this would be a repeat of the introduction of democracy in the West, a long slow retreat for kings and dictators, fear that success for the Idealists will mean the erasure of their way of life.<br /> <br /> It's a subtle war for reality in the vast expanses of space.</div> </div> </div> </div>Alien, strangely enough, is closer to Cyberpunk than to Space Opera. What you describe is almost like a war between a society out of a Cyberpunk setting and a society out of a NFSO setting. That's not a criticism in the slightest, its a good setting idea. I just don't know what I would call it or what themes I would play up.<br /> <br /> It reminds me though of an observation about the eras of Star Trek and how they map to the level of communication we had in the real world when the shows/movies came out. That connection to the real world isn't really important here but the distinctions between the eras is.<br /> <br /> Star Trek TOS is a setting where travel is difficult, settlements are small and isolated, and backup is a long way away. The Enterprise is one part an exploratory craft, one part military vessel pulling into a remote port for shore leave, and one part US Marshal riding through town. Events tend to be contained to the local area and communities are small. Socially, Kirk and crew sit at the tension point between the ideals of the Federation and the practical reality of wherever they happen to be. <br /> <br /> Though it isn't as overt as Firefly, TOS is a space western.<br /> <br /> In TNG, on the other hand, travel is much easier and the galaxy more developed and interconnected. The Enterprise D is usually capable of calling up HQ on short notice and frequently visits established planets and civilizations on diplomatic missions. And Picard and crew aren't just representatives of Federation ideals, the ship is big enough that it is an actual example of Federation society. The show is much more about living in this new society and exploring what that means and where it is difficult.<br /> <br /> TNG is closer to what we think of as Space Opera.<br /> <br /> If I had to choose, I'd say Space Western is the better setting for a struggle between idealism and cynicism. The more isolated setting gives room for the cynical elements to run amok and also leverage to strongarm planets and stations. It also puts the players in a situation where they are the ones who have to deal with the situation because the elders are too far away to reasonably help.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Mage: The Ice-ension: An Epic Game of Reality on the Rink","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/758-ramnesis","name":"Ramnesis","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar758_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2220"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-11-07T16:27:41-08:00","id":1545539,"title":"","content":"I agree with many of your points Ramnesis. I was thinking more of ST:tOS than ST:tNG. But then I normally do. I'm sixty, I remember when no one had to say Original Series. <br /> <br /> The thing that sparked my idea was a discussion of Brighthonorable fantasy versus Grimdark. The idea of two types of society in conflict seemed rich in drama.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/857-astromancer","name":"Astromancer","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar857_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2195"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-11-08T09:22:45-08:00","id":1545574,"title":"","content":"Well the way I would develop it as a Mage scenario is to break out of the current Traditions/Technocracy divide into a conflict of more general ideals.<br /> <br /> What I think would happen is that mages would initially divide themselves up by approaches as they spread out. The Traditions and Technocracy as organizations would dissolve as space kept them out of contact with each other, especially the Traditions. The Trads, Conventions, and Crafts would start down the following approaches:<br /> <br /> There would be the <b>Old Guard</b> that would set up colonies that more or less represented their beliefs. A few of these grow into distant core worlds for the setting. There would also be a handful of smaller but well established worlds.<br /> <br /> There would be the <b>Utopians</b> who tried to institute their experimental societies. Some would succeed as thriving worlds, others would fall into dystopian nightmares.<br /> <br /> Then there would be the ones who weren't interested in spreading their current society, which would include the <b>Adapters</b> who sought to integrate into the worlds they found (e.g. a group of shamans more interested in learning the spiritual ecology of a new planet than holding on to their traditions), the <b>Isolators</b> who retreated from society to focus on their specific goals, and the <b>Explorers</b> who stayed on the move.<br /> <br /> None of these are meant to be factions, by the way, just movements to determine what is out there and how it is set up. I don't see a clean division of each Tradition and Convention into one of these categories, though the majority may favor a certain way.<br /> <br /> <br /> Fast forward to several generations later. The colonies are growing big enough to expand out again and travel is getting just a bit easier. Just enough that interstellar organizations can form, but difficult enough that it takes a lot of investment.<br /> <br /> That's the point that this scenario can get started, because the struggle is between the various organizations that start growing out or connecting all these disparate worlds. Who they are, why they want to do this, and what they hope to accomplish are the core questions. That should give good grounds for both bright and noble factions and dark, rapacious ones.<br /> <br /> My first thoughts for themes is Ideals vs Idealism or perhaps Ideals vs Ideology. The clash between what is right and the system that needs to be fed.<br /> <br /> The details of what ideals are out there and what ideologies are spreading would evolve from which mages joined each of the above movements and which colonies/outposts/endeavors were successful.<br /> <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Mage: The Ice-ension: An Epic Game of Reality on the Rink","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/758-ramnesis","name":"Ramnesis","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar758_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2220"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-11-12T21:31:00-08:00","id":1545772,"title":"","content":"Regarding a Space Opera setting, my personal inclination would be to avoid &quot;future Earth&quot; settings akin to Star Trek, Babylon 5, Firefly, or the like. I feel a better option would be the &quot;a long time ago, in a galaxy far far away&quot; style of Star Wars or the &quot;so far into the future that no one really remembers anything about Earth anymore&quot; of Dune, Foundation, and Fading Suns.<br /> <br /> This would be a vast setting of countless star systems, the majority of its denizens being humans, and their various cultures and political blocs would be based on the various factions from the Traditions and Crafts. The Confederation of Hermes and its various noble houses. The Wyckan worlds. The worlds of the Dragon Emperor. The Batini. Etc. And this has all been around for as long as anyone remembers. But now there is this new Galactic Empire that has been busy conquering everything they come across with their massive star ships, legions of cloned storm troopers, hordes of Autocthonian battle droids, and secret Ksirafai assassins. The various traditional worlds must either come together as a resistance or gradually fall one by one.<br /> <br /> Basically, Mage the Sorcerers Crusade set against a backdrop of a thousand worlds instead of just the one.<br /> <br /> Tech would be about the same as early Star Wars, but entirely based on magic and technomagic. Think the Artificers Handbook turned up to eleven.<br /> <br /> Aliens would probably be based on various Changeling kiths and Bygones.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-11-13T07:23:26-08:00","id":1545787,"title":"","content":"That would be the Far Future Space Opera setting (entry D in the original post). I actually would take that one even farther and drop most of the remnants of the Traditions, Conventions, and Crafts. Maybe the occasional reference to them, in the form of a colony name only historians know the reason for. If no one remembers the earth anymore then so many factions and paradigms have come and gone that even among mages they are lost to time. Some of the factions might still be around in some form, especially if we are inclined to keep around major religions, but even they wouldn't really identify with the classic Traditions.<br /> <br /> For this scenario, I'm inclined to have many of the aliens be like humanity, in that they have sleepers, an impact on the consensus, and mages of their own. Again, if Earth is forgotten, it makes sense to really widen the net for possible paradigms, mages, and respective origins. It's not necessary, of course, but I think it is a potential goldmine we don't see in other scenarios. It also turns the aliens into something that can't just be pushed out of reality by local consensus.<br /> <br /> On the other hand, if aliens are effectively just another form of humans, like in Star Wars, the distinction doesn't matter much.<br /> <br /> The big question is, what kind of Paradigm War or Ascension War is possible and thematic for this time?<br /> <br /> ----<br /> <br /> On another note, one idea that just occurred to me is that the factions from the Time War scenario can be factions that originated in the other scenarios that are in an existential struggle for whose reality is going to exist.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Mage: The Ice-ension: An Epic Game of Reality on the Rink","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/758-ramnesis","name":"Ramnesis","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar758_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2220"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-11-13T15:17:38-08:00","id":1545818,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>No One of Consequence</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1545138-a-sci-fi-scenario-book-which-scenarios?p=1545772#post1545772\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Regarding a Space Opera setting, my personal inclination would be to avoid &quot;future Earth&quot; settings akin to Star Trek, Babylon 5, Firefly, or the like. I feel a better option would be the &quot;a long time ago, in a galaxy far far away&quot; style of Star Wars or the &quot;so far into the future that no one really remembers anything about Earth anymore&quot; of Dune, Foundation, and Fading Suns.<br /> <br /> This would be a vast setting of countless star systems, the majority of its denizens being humans, and their various cultures and political blocs would be based on the various factions from the Traditions and Crafts. The Confederation of Hermes and its various noble houses. The Wyckan worlds. The worlds of the Dragon Emperor. The Batini. Etc. And this has all been around for as long as anyone remembers. But now there is this new Galactic Empire that has been busy conquering everything they come across with their massive star ships, legions of cloned storm troopers, hordes of Autocthonian battle droids, and secret Ksirafai assassins. The various traditional worlds must either come together as a resistance or gradually fall one by one.<br /> <br /> Basically, Mage the Sorcerers Crusade set against a backdrop of a thousand worlds instead of just the one.<br /> <br /> Tech would be about the same as early Star Wars, but entirely based on magic and technomagic. Think the Artificers Handbook turned up to eleven.<br /> <br /> Aliens would probably be based on various Changeling kiths and Bygones.</div> </div> </div> </div>This is an ideal far future setting. I'd prefer the local year to be 5,300AD (as far into the future as the Trojan War is in the past.) Even if you had a ship that could cross the galaxy in a day searching the volume of the Galaxy would take millennia. You could never have a true up to date chart of the galaxy that had relevant borders and social data. The unknown would always be available. No map would lack white spaces beyond knowledge. Every campaign would be a known space campaign.<br /> <br /> Earth, for most of the galaxy, would be as obscure as the pre-dynastic pharaohs of Egypt. So with Earth a folktale or at best a quick reference in a &quot;stones and bones&quot; lecture in a few history courses it really the same as a &quot;galaxy far far away.&quot;","signature":"","edited":{"userid":857,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/857-astromancer","user":"Astromancer","date":". 11-13-2024, 06:21 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/857-astromancer","name":"Astromancer","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar857_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2195"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-11-13T15:26:07-08:00","id":1545819,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ramnesis</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1545138-a-sci-fi-scenario-book-which-scenarios?p=1545787#post1545787\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">That would be the Far Future Space Opera setting (entry D in the original post). I actually would take that one even farther and drop most of the remnants of the Traditions, Conventions, and Crafts. Maybe the occasional reference to them, in the form of a colony name only historians know the reason for. If no one remembers the earth anymore then so many factions and paradigms have come and gone that even among mages they are lost to time. Some of the factions might still be around in some form, especially if we are inclined to keep around major religions, but even they wouldn't really identify with the classic Traditions.<br /> <br /> For this scenario, I'm inclined to have many of the aliens be like humanity, in that they have sleepers, an impact on the consensus, and mages of their own. Again, if Earth is forgotten, it makes sense to really widen the net for possible paradigms, mages, and respective origins. It's not necessary, of course, but I think it is a potential goldmine we don't see in other scenarios. It also turns the aliens into something that can't just be pushed out of reality by local consensus.<br /> <br /> On the other hand, if aliens are effectively just another form of humans, like in Star Wars, the distinction doesn't matter much.<br /> <br /> The big question is, what kind of Paradigm War or Ascension War is possible and thematic for this time?<br /> <br /> ----<br /> <br /> On another note, one idea that just occurred to me is that the factions from the Time War scenario can be factions that originated in the other scenarios that are in an existential struggle for whose reality is going to exist.</div> </div> </div> </div>I would assume that, given the vastness of the galaxy, some areas would have ferocious Ascension Wars, others would find the very idea trite and hinderence to ascension. Some areas would have groups seemingly like the Trads, Cons, and Crafts we know. Others would evolve something new. In so vast a setting everything is going on somewhere.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":857,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/857-astromancer","user":"Astromancer","date":". 03-28-2026, 09:50 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/857-astromancer","name":"Astromancer","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar857_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2195"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-11-13T18:51:56-08:00","id":1545827,"title":"","content":"I'd go even further, and have it be at least two or three &quot;cycles&quot; of rises and falls of galactic civilizations, similar to things like Dune and Foundation.<br /> <br /> Space travel would probably be the purview of special &quot;guilds&quot; using Connection magic to create star-gates or fold space. Exactly how this would work would vary from society to society. The Hermetic worlds, for example, would be very feudalistic and each House having its own area of influence, and in this case House Mercere would probably run most of their interstellar trade, with House Verditius building star ships and House Jerbiton handling most of their diplomacy. Ahl I Batin worlds would have their trade and travel run by the Silk Cartel. I'm not really sure what the equivalent would be for other regions.<br /> <br /> Alien races would probably be handled using the rules from The Bygone Bestiary, and would tend to reflect the style appropriate to their area. Things akin to various faerie beings, homunculi, djinn, kappa, yeti, and what have you.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-11-13T19:25:37-08:00","id":1545829,"title":"","content":"So, if you posit the Far Future setting as something generally Tradition focused (the Traditions as the &quot;good guys&quot; of a space fantasy/space opera standing against a hostile totalitarian empire), I'd also suggest the following:<br /> <br /> Mid Future Space Western - The Technocracy centered setting built around ideas similar to Star Trek or The Orville. The PCs are part of some sort of interstellar space military going out into the frontiers of space and heroically imposing their own self-aggrandizing ideals of progress and civilization. Adjust the levels of smug self-righteousness to taste.<br /> <br /> Near Future Quasi-Apocalypse - Not the post-apocalypse of The Road Warrior or Fallout, but the slow breakdown of society that you see in things like the original Mad Max or Children of Men. The masses have simply lost faith in the various pillars of society such as governments, businesses, law, media, and the like. It's a slow collapse, with the world essentially turning into small isolated enclaves among a vast sea of lawlessness. Even the cities, such as they still exist, resemble some of the worst aspects of the cyberpunk genre. This isn't a world of Traditions or Technocrats. It's one of Orphans (mages or sorcerers) and the occasional Craft (such as the Hollow Ones, Knights Templar, or the Bata'a), scraping by the best they can and maybe trying to rekindle hope among the ruins.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-11-14T06:36:06-08:00","id":1545851,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>No One of Consequence</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1545138-a-sci-fi-scenario-book-which-scenarios?p=1545829#post1545829\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">So, if you posit the Far Future setting as something generally Tradition focused (the Traditions as the &quot;good guys&quot; of a space fantasy/space opera standing against a hostile totalitarian empire), I'd also suggest the following:</div> </div> </div> </div>I still recommend against directly involving the current Traditions and Conventions in anything other than the Cyberpunk and Time Travel settings. It's an artificial restriction on history and setting that doesn't need to be there. Plus, the current fight between the two lends itself to a particular themes: Freedom vs Security, Individuality vs Communalism, etc. I think each scenario should be the opportunity to explore a different kind of philosophical/metaphysical/existential struggle.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Mid Future Space Western - The Technocracy centered setting built around ideas similar to Star Trek or The Orville. The PCs are part of some sort of interstellar space military going out into the frontiers of space and heroically imposing their own self-aggrandizing ideals of progress and civilization. Adjust the levels of smug self-righteousness to taste. </div> </div> </div>Indeed, there is a lot of potential in a space game where the players are among the few that ride between outposts. Its one where the players are pushed to the forefront of whatever ideological struggle is going on, because they are on the front lines and the technical leaders are too far away to interfere.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Near Future Quasi-Apocalypse - Not the post-apocalypse of The Road Warrior or Fallout, but the slow breakdown of society that you see in things like the original Mad Max or Children of Men. The masses have simply lost faith in the various pillars of society such as governments, businesses, law, media, and the like. It's a slow collapse, with the world essentially turning into small isolated enclaves among a vast sea of lawlessness. Even the cities, such as they still exist, resemble some of the worst aspects of the cyberpunk genre. This isn't a world of Traditions or Technocrats. It's one of Orphans (mages or sorcerers) and the occasional Craft (such as the Hollow Ones, Knights Templar, or the Bata'a), scraping by the best they can and maybe trying to rekindle hope among the ruins. </div> </div> </div>I like this one. It reminds me a bit of the Dark Tower series and how society slowly slid into ruin after the world &quot;moved on.&quot; The decay was both social and metaphysical and that's a very good fit for Mage.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Mage: The Ice-ension: An Epic Game of Reality on the Rink","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/758-ramnesis","name":"Ramnesis","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar758_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2220"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-11-18T15:23:38-08:00","id":1546094,"title":"","content":"A point to make about any space setting is Paradox. According to the <b>Players Guide </b>before there were five million people on Earth Magick was far easier. Five million, fifty million, and three hundred million, were population numbers that changed the difficulty of Magic. Paradox came in slowly and in high population density areas first. <br /> <br /> This suggests that a recently colonized world with a population of a few thousand to a few hundred thousand would lack Paradox and Magick would be far less difficult. Meanwhile, on a world settled for a few millennia with a population of tens of billions Vulgar Magick might not be possible. <br /> <br /> Reactions?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/857-astromancer","name":"Astromancer","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar857_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2195"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}